
# Agent worktrees are scratch checkouts and are never committed. Without this
# rule a single `git add -A` stages whatever a tool happened to leave inside
# one, including local credential files that are meant to stay on disk, and the
# only backstop is a secret scan that fires AFTER the commit already exists.
#
# Narrow ON PURPOSE: this matches the worktree directory and nothing else. A
# broad `.claude/` rule would silently untrack files that ARE meant to be
# committed, such as CLAUDE.md and settings.json.
.claude/worktrees/

# Python bytecode from running the .github/scripts test suites in place. The
# tests are run directly (`python3 .github/scripts/<name>_test.py`), which
# writes __pycache__ next to the script, and a `git add -A` then stages it.
__pycache__/

# The lane runner's intent summary. The runner writes it into the worktree
# before the commit, reads it for the pull-request body, and never commits it.
#
# Narrow ON PURPOSE, same reasoning as .claude/worktrees/ above: `.loom/` also
# holds `validation.conf`, which IS meant to be committed, so this names the
# one file rather than the directory.
.loom/summary.md
